# Normalise line endings in the repository, and check them out as LF everywhere.
#
# Without this, Git's core.autocrlf=true (the Windows default) hands Windows
# working copies CRLF while the repository and every Linux checkout hold LF.
# For most files that is invisible. For migrations it is not: scripts/migrate.mjs
# checksums each file's bytes and refuses to run when an applied migration's
# checksum no longer matches. A migration applied from a Windows checkout records
# the CRLF hash, the Vercel build computes the LF hash, and every deploy fails
# with "already applied but its contents have changed" — for a file nobody
# edited.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than plain `text` so the working copy matches the repository on
# every platform, which is what makes the checksum stable no matter where a
# migration is applied from.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files must never be touched.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.xlsx binary
*.xls  binary
*.zip  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
